{"chunk_id": "af8c2237eea7", "text": "At dawn the royal palace follows its daily ritual: bards, musicians, brahmins, and attendants gather to awaken the king with praises, music, and ceremony. Servants prepare perfumes, oils, and golden vessels for the king\u2019s morning ablutions while the entire court waits for him to rise. When he does not appear, the women of the palace attempt to wake him. Touching his body, they realize with growing dread that he shows no sign of life. Their fear turns to certainty: the king has died during the night. The palace, once filled with praise and music, becomes a place of shock and grief. Queen Kaushalya and Queen Sumitra, already broken by the exile of their sons, lie overwhelmed by sorrow, their faded splendor compared to stars hidden behind clouds. The scene marks the tragic consequence of Rama\u2019s exile and the collapse of royal order in Ayodhya.\nPalace attendants arrive to awaken the king with music and ritual, only to discover he has died, plunging the palace into grief.\nThemes: royal death, palace ritual, sudden tragedy, grief of queens", "vector": [0.010643686167895794, -0.006073083728551865, -0.0033722734078764915, -0.011029278859496117, -0.008044259622693062, 0.01990455761551857, -0.021234186366200447, -0.0017335048178210855, -0.015357223339378834, -0.014240334741771221, 0.007798278704285622, 0.0026725558564066887, 0.0044542597606778145, 0.0045805745758116245, -0.0213006678968668, 0.0015257501509040594, 0.0468827448785305, -0.0061495378613471985, 0.02535603940486908, -0.009380538016557693, 0.002449842868372798, 0.002757319714874029, -0.003206069814041257, 0.027164336293935776, -0.00033947109477594495, 0.007971130311489105, 0.00598000967875123, -0.03145904093980789, 0.01171403843909502, -0.0038060653023421764, -0.0030598104931414127, -0.021699557080864906, -0.026605891063809395, -0.00823705643415451, -0.008935111574828625, 0.0009074723348021507, 0.0025512271095067263, -0.020143890753388405, 0.00844979751855135, -0.005